\name{readers_identify}
\alias{readers_identify}
\title{Identify a result file without parsing its contents}
\usage{
readers_identify(path, timeout = 60)
}
\arguments{
\item{path}{Path to a result file.}

\item{timeout}{Seconds to allow, or \code{NULL} to wait indefinitely.
}

}
\description{
Cheap by design: mzLib resolves the type and stops, so identifying a million-row file costs no
more than identifying an empty one.
}
\details{
It is not, however, *pure*. mzLib disambiguates a bare \code{.tsv} by reading its first line, a
\code{.mztab} by its first five, and a Bruker \code{.d} by which analysis file the directory holds, so
an unreadable file raises rather than returning a guess.
}
\value{
An \code{mzlibr_file_info}: \code{path}, \code{file_type}, \code{extension}, \code{reader}, \code{views} and
  \code{is_quantifiable}.

}
\section{Ask this before quantifying}{


\code{is_quantifiable} is the precondition for \code{\link{flashlfq_quantify}} — when \code{FALSE}, mzLib can
still read the file, it simply has no uniform view, and quantification would fail on it.

\strong{But \code{TRUE} is not permission.} It reports what mzLib's *interface* offers, not that the
numbers are comparable. \code{MsFraggerPsm} is quantifiable by interface and should not be
quantified: among other things its retention times are in seconds while MetaMorpheus's are in
minutes, and mzLib does not normalise them. See \code{\link{readers_read_results}} and the \code{caveats} it
returns.

}
